%% Tenant customer authentication and session lifecycle
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Customer
    participant Web as Customer Web/BFF
    participant API as Tenant Auth API
    participant Context as Tenant resolver/context
    participant Auth as AuthService and OTP service
    participant DB as Tenant PostgreSQL
    participant Queue as Email/OTP queue
    participant Mail as Email or OTP provider

    Customer->>Web: Register or submit login on tenant host
    Web->>API: POST /auth/register or /auth/login
    API->>Context: Resolve tenant from trusted host
    Context-->>API: Immutable organization context

    alt Registration
        API->>Auth: Validate input and create tenant user
        Auth->>DB: Store user and one-time verification state
        Auth->>Queue: Enqueue verification message
        Queue->>Mail: Deliver verification message
        Mail-->>Customer: Verification code/link
        Customer->>Web: Submit verification code
        Web->>API: POST /auth/verify-email
        API->>Auth: Consume one-time code
        Auth->>DB: Mark email verified
    else Login
        API->>Auth: Normalize identifier and rate-limit attempt
        Auth->>DB: Read tenant user and lock state
        Auth->>Auth: Verify password and account policy
    end

    Auth->>DB: Create refresh session and token state
    Auth-->>Web: Short-lived access token + HTTP-only refresh cookie
    Web-->>Customer: Authenticated tenant session

    Customer->>Web: Continue after access-token expiry
    Web->>API: POST /auth/refresh with refresh cookie
    API->>Auth: Validate version, rotation, and revocation state
    Auth->>DB: Rotate refresh session state
    Auth-->>Web: New access token and refresh cookie

    Customer->>Web: Logout or reset password
    Web->>API: Logout or OTP/password-reset request
    API->>Auth: Revoke session or consume reset state
    Auth->>DB: Persist revocation/reset transition
    Web-->>Customer: Cleared or replaced session

    Note over Context,DB: The browser's organization ID is never proof of tenant identity.
    Note over Auth,Mail: OTP and recovery paths are one-time, attempt-limited, rate-limited, and enumeration-safe.
